<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- The authoritative record of an AI Accountant job: what was authorised, by whom, in which company,
    over which records, and how far each one got.

    WHY IT IS IN THE DATABASE. The same state already travels in the conversation blob the browser stores
    and returns, and that blob is the only copy today. It is unusable as an authorisation: it is held by
    the client, so a second tab knows nothing of the first, a reload can lose it, and what comes back is
    whatever the browser chose to send. For deciding what a write may do, the server has to hold the copy
    that counts.

    The blob does not go away — it still carries the job across turns and is what makes the assistant
    responsive without a lookup. This row is what the server BELIEVES; a disagreement is resolved in favour
    of this one, and the blob is treated as a hint.

    SCOPE. One active row per (user, company, conversation). An authorisation is given by a person, inside
    one set of books, in one chat panel, and no part is inferable from the others: the same user in two
    companies is two jobs, the same company under two users is two authorisations, and the same user and
    company in two browser tabs is two conversations that must not be able to continue each other's work.

    CONCURRENCY. Axelor gives every entity a `version` column and JPA checks it on update, which is exactly
    the guard two tabs need — the second write of a stale copy fails rather than silently overwriting the
    first. AiWorkflowStore catches that, re-reads and re-applies; see its javadoc for why re-applying is
    safe (every update is a union or a state read from the database, never a replacement).

    The record lists themselves are held as JSON rather than as child rows. They are read and written whole,
    exactly once per turn, by one owner; splitting them into a table would buy queryability nobody has asked
    for and cost a join, a cascade and a second place for the same facts to live. -->
  <entity name="AiWorkflowState">

    <!-- Who and where. Both required for an authorisation to mean anything. -->
    <!-- `column` is explicit because `user` is an SQL keyword and the generator refuses to derive a
      column of that name. Stock does the same wherever it holds a User (ImportHistory, FECImport). -->
    <many-to-one name="user" ref="com.axelor.auth.db.User" title="User" required="true"
      index="true" column="user_id"/>
    <many-to-one name="company" ref="com.axelor.apps.base.db.Company" title="Company"
      required="true" index="true"/>

    <!-- Identity of the job, minted when it opens and never reused. It is the ONLY thing that decides
      whether two writes to this row are two turns of one job (merge their progress) or two different
      jobs (never merge — their authorisations were each given for something specific). The request,
      the goal and the task all repeat across jobs and so distinguish nothing. -->
    <string name="workflowId" title="Workflow" index="true"/>

    <!-- Which chat panel authorised this job. Part of the key, beside the user and the company: two
      browser tabs are one user in one company, and before this they shared the row — so a job
      authorised by a sentence typed in one tab could be continued by «продължи» typed in another.
      Minted by the client, opaque here, only ever compared. See AiConversationKey. -->
    <string name="conversationId" title="Conversation" index="true"/>

    <!-- 1 ACTIVE, 2 SETTLED (every target done or blocked), 3 ABANDONED (superseded, or the company
      changed under it). Only an ACTIVE row is ever resumed. -->
    <integer name="statusSelect" title="Status" default="1" index="true"/>

    <!-- The route this job was opened under, as the closed vocabularies name it. Kept as columns rather
      than only inside the JSON so a job can be found and counted without parsing it. -->
    <string name="task" title="Task"/>
    <string name="goal" title="Goal"/>
    <string name="routeDomain" title="Domain"/>
    <string name="routeScope" title="Scope"/>
    <string name="explicitness" title="Explicitness"/>

    <!-- The user's own authorising words, verbatim and never re-interpreted. This is the audit trail:
      what a person actually said, beside what was done under it. -->
    <string name="request" title="Request" large="true"/>

    <!-- The serialised AiWorkflow: targets, completed, and per-record blocker class, blocker code and
      reason. One document because it is one fact. -->
    <string name="stateJson" title="State" large="true"/>

    <!-- Counters, for the metric line and for answering "how long has this been open" without diffing
      two JSON documents. -->
    <integer name="targetCount" title="Targets" default="0"/>
    <integer name="completedCount" title="Completed" default="0"/>
    <integer name="blockedCount" title="Blocked" default="0"/>
    <integer name="turnCount" title="Turns" default="0"/>

    <datetime name="lastTurnDateTime" title="Last turn"/>

    <!-- The three statusSelect values live in AiWorkflowStore as constants. An `extra-code` block here
      would be the natural home, but this platform version does not emit one into the generated entity
      (stock domains that declare one come out without their constants too), so code referring to them
      through the entity does not compile. Keep the two in step: 1 ACTIVE, 2 SETTLED, 3 ABANDONED. -->

  </entity>

</domain-models>
